  "response": "Inquiry into the loss of the battleship began within days of the disaster. After the USS Maine exploded and sank in Havana harbor on February 15, 1898, the U.S. Navy convened a court of inquiry headed by Captain William T. Sampson in 1898. Sampson, an ordnance specialist with long experience of naval explosives, directed the collection of testimony from surviving officers and the technical survey of the wreck, which lay in shallow water with its forward section shattered. Whereas Sampson led that substantive work, the 1898 court of inquiry was presided over by Admiral George Dewey, whose seniority lent the proceedings formal authority within the service.\n\nThe court's reasoning depended on what could be seen beneath the surface of the harbor. Divers reported that a section of the Maine's keel near frame 18 was bent into an inverted V shape, and the members took this deformation to indicate force driven upward from beneath the ship rather than outward from within it. On the strength of that keel evidence, the Sampson court of inquiry concluded that a submarine mine had set off the Maine's forward magazines, although it declined to assign responsibility to any party. The verdict did not stand apart from politics. It was a consequence of the United States declaring war on Spain in April 1898, a decision whose pressure shaped how the court framed its findings and how the public received them.\n\nLater investigators shifted attention from the wreck's exterior to the ship's internal arrangement. Their reconstructions identified two mechanisms, and at least one of them, perhaps both, had been at work. On one side, because the Maine's coal bunkers lay adjacent to its forward magazines, a spontaneous fire was burning in a coal bunker beside the forward magazine, a hazard familiar from other warships that carried bituminous coal in the period. On the other, a mine detonated against the hull below the forward magazines, as the original court had maintained. The dispute therefore turned on the location of the initiating blast. Either, despite the Sampson court's mine verdict, the explosion that destroyed the Maine originated inside the ship's hull, or it originated outside the ship's hull; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold.\n\nThe decisive reassessment came from engineering analysis rather than fresh salvage. A second board had examined the hulk after it was raised in 1911, but its report largely preserved the mine thesis while revising the point of detonation. Admiral Hyman G. Rickover later commissioned a reconstruction that drew on the photographic and documentary record of both earlier inquiries, together with modern understanding of how underwater and internal explosions deform steel. The Rickover study was published in 1958, whereas its findings rested on that much fuller technical foundation: it concluded that the damage was most consistent with an internal coal-bunker fire igniting a magazine. That conclusion settled the conflict between the mine verdict of 1898 and the case for an origin within the ship, because it explained the inverted keel as the product of a magazine blast buckling the structure rather than as the mark of an external charge.\n\nHistoriography has since followed the engineers. Most historians now attribute the sinking of the Maine to an internal explosion; in other words, the prevailing view among historians is that the Maine was destroyed from within. That consensus rests less on any single document than on the convergence of structural analysis with the ship's design, in which fuel and munitions were stored side by side behind thin bulkheads. It has also reshaped how the war's origins are taught. The cry of \"Remember the Maine\" now serves less as evidence of Spanish hostility than as an illustration of how a technically uncertain finding, issued under political strain, can harden into a justification for armed conflict.",
